{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794856351,"nonce":"b1VKLlwtgSc7HmO1I+pAuAV9zGA=","title":"A native thread that blocks forever, with too few thread agents, ends in \"out of memory allocating an array\"","message":"Reported by hxn (hxn-bd session, 2026-11-16), not reproduced here. hxn-gpu-webgpu at 069e57d starts a Rust\nstd::thread (wasi thread-spawn) that loops on `memory.atomic.wait32(word, seen, -1)` and calls a settle\nfunction when the word changes. In headless Chrome under Ash 0.1.20 it passes with the machine's own core\ncount and with HXN_PAGE_CORES=2; with HXN_PAGE_CORES=3, and on GitHub's macOS runners, the program dies\nright after its first await: \"[ash] out of memory allocating an array / heap cap 512 MB, live 439 MB,\nafter 7 collection(s)\".\n\nReproducer: `HXN_BROWSER_ARGS=--enable-unsafe-webgpu HXN_PAGE_CORES=3 hxn check hl --wasm --threads --browser`\nwith hxn 0.3.6 on that commit.\n\nGuess from hxn: with few thread agents the thread lands on the main scheduler (worker.js `spawn` answers\n\"no idle agent, running on the main scheduler\"), where a wait cannot block, so it spins and something per\nswitch is kept. To check: what a fiber's memory.atomic.wait32 does on the main scheduler, and what\nallocates per switch. Whether a thread the program did not ask for as a Haxe thread should be started on\nthe scheduler at all, or fail its spawn when no agent is free, is a design question: a failed spawn is an\nerror the library can handle, a spin is not.","files":null}]}